1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,791
A tenant records its content types, parts, and fields in a content definition.

2
00:00:05,791 --> 00:00:10,638
The database keeps it by default, and the File Content Definition feature keeps

3
00:00:10,638 --> 00:00:11,722
it in a file.

4
00:00:11,722 --> 00:00:16,170
Let's see how the store is chosen, and how to move back to the database.

5
00:00:20,770 --> 00:00:24,854
A content definition is one document, a Content Definition Record.

6
00:00:24,854 --> 00:00:28,881
It holds the type records and the part records of the tenant, and

7
00:00:28,881 --> 00:00:32,000
each part record holds the records of its fields.

8
00:00:32,750 --> 00:00:36,399
The content definition manager never touches storage itself.

9
00:00:36,399 --> 00:00:40,766
It goes through an I Content Definition Store, which loads the record for

10
00:00:40,766 --> 00:00:44,580
updating, gets a cached copy for reading, and saves the record.

11
00:00:45,330 --> 00:00:48,146
The default implementation is the database store.

12
00:00:48,146 --> 00:00:51,833
It asks a Document Manager for the record, which reads it from the

13
00:00:51,833 --> 00:00:53,830
database and keeps a cached copy.

14
00:00:58,030 --> 00:01:01,588
You enable File Content Definition in Tools, Features.

15
00:01:01,588 --> 00:01:06,214
The Contents module declares the feature in its manifest, and gives it a

16
00:01:06,214 --> 00:01:10,010
startup class that runs only while the feature is enabled.

17
00:01:10,760 --> 00:01:14,605
The database store is added only if no store is registered.

18
00:01:14,605 --> 00:01:20,342
The feature's startup calls Add File Content Definition Store, which removes the registered

19
00:01:20,342 --> 00:01:23,240
store and adds the file store in its place.

20
00:01:23,990 --> 00:01:28,931
The file store has the same three methods, but its Document Manager uses

21
00:01:28,931 --> 00:01:30,832
the file document store.

22
00:01:30,832 --> 00:01:36,216
The record names its file, so each tenant gets a ContentDefinition.json file in

23
00:01:36,216 --> 00:01:38,750
its folder, under App_Data, Sites.

24
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:42,477
The file holds the same record as indented JSON.

25
00:01:42,477 --> 00:01:47,548
The admin, migrations, and recipes all change definitions through the manager, so each

26
00:01:47,548 --> 00:01:50,470
change is written to the file after the commit.

27
00:01:51,220 --> 00:01:55,689
Without a file, the store returns an empty record, and enabling the feature

28
00:01:55,689 --> 00:01:57,841
copies nothing from the database.

29
00:01:57,841 --> 00:02:02,476
To start from those definitions, export them with a deployment plan first, and

30
00:02:02,476 --> 00:02:05,400
import the package once the feature is enabled.

31
00:02:09,669 --> 00:02:15,332
The documentation suggests the file for development, and the database for production.

32
00:02:15,332 --> 00:02:18,071
To move, first export the definitions.

33
00:02:18,071 --> 00:02:24,979
In Tools, Deployments, Plans, choose Add Deployment Plan, name it Content Definitions, and select Create.

34
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:29,996
On the plan, choose Add Step, and add Update Content Definitions.

35
00:02:29,996 --> 00:02:36,220
In the step form, check Include all content types and parts definitions, and select Create.

36
00:02:36,702 --> 00:02:39,310
Choose Execute, and select File Download.

37
00:02:39,310 --> 00:02:43,582
The step reads the definitions from the active store, so it exports the

38
00:02:43,582 --> 00:02:47,522
file's content, and the browser downloads ContentDefinitions.zip.

39
00:02:49,159 --> 00:02:53,064
Next, in Tools, Features, find File Content Definition.

40
00:02:53,064 --> 00:02:55,817
Select Disable, and confirm with Yes.

41
00:02:55,817 --> 00:03:01,259
The tenant restarts with the database store, and the feature shows as disabled.

42
00:03:02,290 --> 00:03:07,807
Open Design, Content Definition, Content Types, and search for Event, a type created

43
00:03:07,807 --> 00:03:09,769
while the file was in use.

44
00:03:09,769 --> 00:03:11,301
There is no result:

45
00:03:11,301 --> 00:03:13,570
the database never received it.

46
00:03:15,199 --> 00:03:21,125
Open Tools, Deployments, Package Import, select ContentDefinitions.zip, and select Import.

47
00:03:21,125 --> 00:03:26,496
The package's Content Definition recipe step saves each type and part through the

48
00:03:26,496 --> 00:03:29,459
active store, which is the database again.

49
00:03:29,944 --> 00:03:33,306
The page reports that the deployment package was imported.

50
00:03:33,306 --> 00:03:36,667
Back in Content Types, searching for Event finds the type.

51
00:03:36,667 --> 00:03:40,764
The definitions are in the database, and the site reads them from there.

52
00:03:44,964 --> 00:03:50,187
On the Default tenant, with Tenant Feature Profiles enabled, a feature profile restricts

53
00:03:50,187 --> 00:03:52,577
the features of the tenants given it.

54
00:03:52,577 --> 00:03:57,189
This Feature Profiles recipe step has an Exclude rule that names File Content

55
00:03:57,189 --> 00:04:00,634
Definition by its id, so those tenants cannot enable it.

56
00:04:02,634 --> 00:04:04,805
That's content definition stores:

57
00:04:04,805 --> 00:04:09,814
the database by default, a ContentDefinition.json file for each tenant with the File

58
00:04:09,814 --> 00:04:14,767
Content Definition feature, and a deployment plan to move definitions between them.

59
00:04:14,767 --> 00:04:16,214
Thanks for watching.

